prelink: round the range in ml_static_mfree instead of asserting

OSKext::unload() frees the load address recorded in the prelinked kext
records.  For the com.apple.kpi.* pseudo-kexts that address is a small offset
from __PRELINK_TEXT, so it is not page aligned and the assert in
ml_static_mfree() fires during the prelink teardown:

  panic: Assertion failed: (vaddr & (PAGE_SIZE - 1)) == 0

Round the range out to whole pages there instead.  The rounded range covers
the original bytes and adds at most one page on each side, which is part of the
same block being torn down, so no memory outside the allocation is released.
A line is printed whenever the rounding happens so the case stays visible.
diff --git a/osfmk/arm64/machine_routines.c b/osfmk/arm64/machine_routines.c
index 91a8a6b..5ca9ea5 100644
--- a/osfmk/arm64/machine_routines.c
+++ b/osfmk/arm64/machine_routines.c
@@ -1810,7 +1810,31 @@ ml_static_mfree(
 		return;
 	}
 
-	assert((vaddr & (PAGE_SIZE - 1)) == 0); /* must be page aligned */
+	/*
+	 * The native-prelink teardown hands us addresses taken straight from the
+	 * prelinked kext records.  The com.apple.kpi.* pseudo-kexts carry small
+	 * offsets from __PRELINK_TEXT as their load address, and OSKext::unload()
+	 * passes those here verbatim, so they are not page aligned:
+	 *
+	 *   panic: Assertion failed: (vaddr & (PAGE_SIZE - 1)) == 0
+	 *
+	 * Round the range out to whole pages instead.  The rounded range covers the
+	 * original bytes and adds at most one page on each side, which belongs to
+	 * the same block being torn down, so nothing outside the allocation is
+	 * released.  A line is printed whenever this happens so it stays visible.
+	 */
+	if (vaddr & (PAGE_SIZE - 1)) {
+		vm_offset_t aligned_start = trunc_page_64(vaddr);
+		vm_offset_t aligned_end   = round_page_64(vaddr + size);
+
+		printf("KEBAB PRELINK: ml_static_mfree %#lx+%#lx not page aligned, "
+		    "rounding to %#lx+%#lx\n",
+		    (unsigned long)vaddr, (unsigned long)size,
+		    (unsigned long)aligned_start,
+		    (unsigned long)(aligned_end - aligned_start));
+		vaddr = aligned_start;
+		size  = aligned_end - aligned_start;
+	}
 
 	for (vaddr_cur = vaddr;
 	    vaddr_cur < trunc_page_64(vaddr + size);
